# Auld Lang Syne, strum and sing (repertoire plan: auld-lang-syne). The traditional Scottish tune
# (printed 1799) with Robert Burns's words of 1788, verse 1 and the chorus, in his Scots. The tune
# is the one independent recollections agreed on note for note, as it is commonly sung today
# (older printings vary). It keeps to the five notes of D major pentatonic (D E F# A B), and
# every line starts with a pickup on beat 4.
# Your part: D (xx0232), A (x02220), G (320003) and the open Bm7 (x20202), the easier shape to
# use until barre chords arrive at level 5. The verse strums four quarter downstrokes a bar; the
# chorus lifts to "down, down-up" on every two beats. Bars 7 and 15 change from Bm7 to A on
# beat 3. The pickup bar is silent for you: the tune's first note comes before your first strum.
# The tune (second track) carries the words, played an octave below the sung pitch, in first
# position on strings 5 to 2 (finger = fret number).
title: Auld Lang Syne
id: auld-lang-syne
composer: Traditional Scottish; words by Robert Burns
source: Traditional tune printed 1799, melody as commonly sung today; words by Robert Burns, 1788, verse 1 and chorus; arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain (traditional tune; Burns died in 1796); this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 3
tags: strumming, new year, scottish, pentatonic, pickup, lyrics
tempo: 80
time: 4/4
key: D
pickup: q

track: Your strum
role: rhythm
